# MakeMeSwitch CLI

Turn a plain Debian/Ubuntu server with a few Ethernet ports into a managed
Layer-2 switch with a Cisco-inspired CLI and a web dashboard.

MakeMeSwitch is **not** a simulator: every `switchport`, `vlan`, `mtu`,
`shutdown` or `ip routing` command is reconciled into real
[Open vSwitch](https://www.openvswitch.org/) state on the running host, and the
tests prove forwarding with real packets in Linux network namespaces.

```
Switch> enable
Switch# configure terminal
Switch(config)# hostname LAB-SW01
LAB-SW01(config)# vlan 10
LAB-SW01(config-vlan)# name SERVERS
LAB-SW01(config-vlan)# exit
LAB-SW01(config)# interface ethernet 1/1
LAB-SW01(config-if)# description core uplink
LAB-SW01(config-if)# switchport mode access
LAB-SW01(config-if)# switchport access vlan 10
LAB-SW01(config-if)# no shutdown
LAB-SW01(config-if)# end
LAB-SW01# write memory
```

- CLI: `sudo makemeswitch`
- Web panel: `http://<switch-ip>:8000/` (binds all interfaces by default; use
  `--https` + `--port 8443` for TLS)

---

## 1. What actually works

| Area | Status |
|---|---|
| L2 switching in OVS (access, trunk, native VLAN, allowed lists) | ✅ implemented |
| VLAN isolation, MAC learning, broadcast/unknown-unicast flooding | ✅ implemented |
| RSTP (per-port edge on access ports), priority | ✅ implemented |
| LACP / static bonds, port-channel config | ✅ implemented |
| SPAN / port mirroring | ✅ implemented |
| MTU per port | ✅ implemented |
| Physical port discovery + stable identity mapping (PCI/USB topology) | ✅ implemented |
| Hotplug / link up-down / speed / duplex / counters | ✅ implemented |
| Running + startup config, `write memory`, `copy run start`, rollback | ✅ implemented |
| Validation, idempotent reconciliation, drift detection, OVS rollback | ✅ implemented |
| Static L3: SVIs on OVS internal ports, `ip routing`, static routes | ✅ implemented |
| REST API + WebSocket stats, Argon2id auth, RBAC, CSRF, audit log | ✅ implemented |
| Web panel (React + TS + Vite + Tailwind), xterm web terminal | ✅ implemented |
| LLDP neighbours | ⚠️ via `lldpd` if installed; otherwise reported as unavailable |
| OSPF/BGP/FRR | ⚠️ backend stub only — no dynamic routing yet |
| ACL, QoS, storm control | ❌ not implemented (not advertised in UI either) |

Anything the host or backend cannot do is reported as *unavailable*, never as a
successful no-op.

## 2. Architecture

```
            ┌──────────────────────────────┐
  SSH ────► │ CLI (prompt_toolkit, parser) │──┐
            └──────────────────────────────┘  │
            ┌──────────────────────────────┐  │   ┌────────────────────┐
 Browser ─► │ FastAPI REST + WebSocket     │──┼──►│  core.engine.Engine │
            │ (auth, RBAC, CSRF, PTY)      │  │   │  validate → diff →  │
            └──────────────────────────────┘  │   │  reconcile → save   │
            ┌──────────────────────────────┐  │   └─────────┬──────────┘
            │ React panel (static assets)  │──┘             │
            └──────────────────────────────┘                │
                                     ┌──────────────────────┼──────────────────────┐
                                     ▼                      ▼                      ▼
                              backends.ovs (ovs-vsctl)  core.l3 (ip/sysctl)   core.store (SQLite)
                                     │                      │                      │
                                 OVSDB + datapath      kernel routing       config/users/audit
```

Design rules enforced in code:

- **Single write path.** CLI and web never call `ovs-vsctl` themselves; both go
  through `Engine`, so they always see the same state (`tests/unit/test_api.py`,
  `tests/unit/test_cli.py`).
- **Declarative + idempotent.** `Engine.plan()` diffs desired vs. actual;
  `apply()` is a no-op when nothing changed.
- **Atomic-ish with rollback.** OVS is snapshotted before an apply; on error the
  snapshot is restored and the running config is left untouched.
- **No shell.** External commands are always argv arrays
  (`subprocess.run([...])`), inputs are validated (`vlan 1-4094`, ifname regex,
  MTU range, IP/prefix checks).
- **No surprises at install.** The installer never enslaves a NIC, never
  touches routes or SSH.

## 3. Requirements

- Debian 12/13 or Ubuntu 22.04/24.04+ (bare metal or VM **with real NICs**)
- Python 3.11+
- Open vSwitch 2.17+ (`openvswitch-switch`)
- `iproute2`, `ethtool`
- Optional: `lldpd` (topology page), `frr` (future dynamic routing)

The physical switch ports can be onboard NICs, PCIe cards or USB Ethernet
adapters. USB adapters work but see [Limitations](#10-limitations).

## 4. Installation

```bash
git clone <your-fork-url>/makemeswitch.git
cd makemeswitch
sudo ./install.sh
```

The clone URL is whatever repository you push this to — the project is
self-contained: no submodules, no reference to a canonical upstream.

After it finishes:

```
Switch# show version
Switch# show interfaces status
```

```bash
sudo makemeswitch                 # local console CLI
ssh admin@<switch-ip>             # switch CLI over SSH (port 22)
ssh <os-user>@<switch-ip> -p 8022 # host shell (OS accounts)
http://<switch-ip>:8000/          # web panel, login admin / MakeMeSwitch
```

> **Port 22 changes hands.** The switch CLI takes 22; the host `sshd` moves to
> 8022. The installer validates 8022 before touching 22, backs up
> `/etc/ssh/sshd_config`, and rolls back if anything fails — but open both
> ports in your firewall *before* you install. Use `--keep-ssh-22` to skip the
> swap entirely. See section 7a for the recovery procedure.

Options:

```bash
sudo ./install.sh --help
  --no-web                 install the CLI/API only, disable the web panel
  --bind ADDR              API/panel bind address (default 0.0.0.0 = LAN)
  --port PORT              API/panel port (default 8000)
  --https                  serve the panel over HTTPS
  --tls-cert PATH          TLS certificate (PEM)
  --tls-key PATH           TLS private key (PEM)
  --admin-user NAME        initial administrator (default admin)
  --admin-password PASS    initial password (default: MakeMeSwitch)
  --with-lldp              install lldpd (default: on)
  --no-attach-all          leave physical NICs unassigned (default: attach all)
  --keep-ssh-22            do not move host SSH to 8022 (switch CLI off port 22)
```

The installer:

1. verifies Debian/Ubuntu and the version,
2. installs `openvswitch-switch`, `frr` (L3), `lldpd` (LLDP), Python venv,
   `iproute2`, `ethtool` — everything needed is installed automatically,
3. copies the app to `/opt/makemeswitch/app` and creates `/opt/makemeswitch/venv`,
4. links the CLI to `/usr/local/bin/makemeswitch` (so `sudo makemeswitch` works),
5. ships the prebuilt panel in `makemeswitch/web/dist`; if a source tree has no
   prebuilt assets it installs `nodejs`/`npm` and builds them, and the installer
   **fails** rather than finishing with a broken UI,
6. installs `makemeswitch.service` (systemd, `CAP_NET_ADMIN` only, read-only `/usr`),
7. writes `/etc/makemeswitch/makemeswitch.yaml`, creates `/var/lib/makemeswitch`,
8. creates the first administrator (default `admin` / `MakeMeSwitch`) and stores it in
   `/root/makemeswitch-initial-credentials.txt` (mode 600),
9. attaches **every physical NIC except the management NIC** to the switch
   (`--no-attach-all` skips this),
10. stages persistent kernel names `Ethernet1`, `Ethernet2`, … for the next boot,
11. starts the service, checks that `/` really returns the panel, and prints the
    LAN URL.

It never enslaves the management NIC (default route / interface holding an IP /
the NIC your SSH session arrives on), never changes the default route, and never
touches NetworkManager/systemd-networkd configuration.

The installer prints the LAN URL, e.g. `http://192.168.1.50:8000/`. Open it from
any device on the management network and sign in with `admin` / `MakeMeSwitch`
(the default; change it with `sudo makemeswitch user passwd admin` or the Users
page). The same credentials are written to
`/root/makemeswitch-initial-credentials.txt`.

To serve TLS on the usual switch port:

```bash
sudo ./install.sh --https --port 8443 --tls-cert /etc/makemeswitch/tls/cert.pem --tls-key /etc/makemeswitch/tls/key.pem
```

If you prefer not to expose the panel, bind it to the management address only
(`--bind 10.0.0.10`) or to `127.0.0.1` and use an SSH tunnel.

## 5. First-time setup

```bash
sudo makemeswitch
```

```
Switch> enable
Switch# show interfaces status           # every NIC is already a switch port
Switch# configure terminal
Switch(config)# hostname LAB-SW01
LAB-SW01(config)# interface ethernet 1
LAB-SW01(config-if)# description uplink to lab
```

Attaching a NIC prints a preview and asks for confirmation, because the NIC
leaves host networking and becomes a switch port:

```
! Attaching Ethernet1/1 (enp1s0) will enslave the NIC into OVS bridge mms0.
! Host networking on enp1s0 stops working until detached.
Proceed? [confirm]
```

The host management interface (the one carrying the default route or an IP
address, and the interface your SSH session arrives on) is **refused**:

```
% enp0s3 carries host management connectivity - refusing to attach
```

Then create VLANs and configure ports as in the example at the top, and finish
with `write memory`.

## 6. Port mapping and identity

`show interfaces mapping` shows the live table:

```
Switch Port   Linux NIC   Identity             Kind   Driver   Link
Ethernet1     enp1s0      pci:0000:01:00.0     pci    e1000e   up
Ethernet2     Ethernet2   usb:1-1.2            usb    ax88179  up
```

- Ports are named `Ethernet1`, `Ethernet2`, … in CLI/GUI and the kernel.
  Version 0.1.5 uses an explicit, locked boot/install oneshot, after OVS and
  network-online and before the API. The installer removes old `.path` and
  `.link` rules. Watching sysfs with `.path` does not reliably implement hotplug
  and can retrigger on the application's own renames.
- Every boot rescans all present NICs, including adapters added after install.
  No fixed NIC count is assumed (256 and 512 simulated ports are tested).
  New ports are attached when `attach_new_nics: true`; with `false` they remain
  unassigned. Immediate hotplug naming is **not** promised; reboot after adding
  adapters. API reads/discovery never rename or bring links down.
- Identity uses the last PCI endpoint in sysfs, not a shared upstream bridge.
  Interrupted `mmstmpN` renames are recovered. Numbering follows physical order;
  saved and running interface configuration follows the NIC, not the number.
- Management NICs with addresses/default routes remain excluded. Renaming is
  the same netdev and retains IPv4; a required down transition can affect IPv6.
  Missing non-link-local addresses are restored, but SLAAC/link-local convergence
  is not guaranteed. Every successful rename is journalled and reversed on
  failure; rollback failures are reported, never hidden.

- `Ethernet1/1` (Cisco slot/port) is still accepted everywhere:
  `interface ethernet 1/1` == `interface eth 1` == `interface Ethernet1`.
- `interface` also works from inside an interface (or a range) — `int eth 3`
  jumps straight to another port, no `exit` needed.
- Identity is derived from sysfs topology: PCI address for onboard/PCIe cards,
  USB bus-port path for USB adapters, and stored in SQLite — independent of the
  kernel name, so a rename never moves a port onto another NIC.
- The mapping is stored in SQLite. A NIC that disappears stays reserved; if it
  comes back at the same topology path it gets the same switch port.
- A *different* device plugged into a previously used USB port does not inherit
  the old port silently — the engine reports a mismatch instead of stealing the
  mapping.

## 7. CLI reference

Modes: `User EXEC (>)` → `Privileged EXEC (#)` → `(config)#` →
`(config-if)#`, `(config-vlan)#`, `(config-svi)#`, `(config-router)#`.

**Typing commands.** Any unambiguous prefix works (`sh int st`, `conf t`,
`int eth1/1`). `Tab` completes, `?` prints context help immediately (also mid
line: `show ?`), history is per user, and `do <exec command>` runs EXEC commands
without leaving configuration mode. Interfaces accept every common spelling:
`interface Ethernet1/1`, `interface eth1/1`, `interface ethernet 1/1`,
`interface 1/1`; SVIs as `interface Vlan10` or `interface vlan 10`.

| Command | Mode | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `enable` / `disable` | exec | privilege level |
| `configure terminal` | priv | global config |
| `hostname NAME` | config | |
| `vlan ID` / `name TEXT` / `no vlan ID` | config | 1–4094, deletion blocked while in use |
| `interface ethernet N/M` | config, config-if | `Ethernet1/1` … (also `int eth 2` from another interface) |
| `interface range SPEC` | config, config-if | `eth1-4`, `eth1,3,5`, `ethernet 1 - 2` |
| `switchport attach LINUX_NIC` / `no switchport attach` | config-if | preview + confirmation |
| `switchport mode access\|trunk` | config-if | |
| `switchport access vlan ID` | config-if | |
| `switchport trunk allowed vlan LIST` | config-if | `10,20,30-40` |
| `switchport trunk native vlan ID` | config-if | |
| `description TEXT` | config-if | |
| `shutdown` / `no shutdown` | config-if | real OVS port disable/enable |
| `mtu N` | config-if | 576–9216 |
| `channel-group N mode active\|passive\|on` | config-if | LACP/static bond |
| `interface port-channel N` | config | create/open a port-channel (may be empty) |
| `monitor session N source\|destination ...` | config | SPAN |
| `spanning-tree mode rstp\|stp\|none`, `spanning-tree priority N` | config | |
| `ip routing` / `no ip routing` | config | L3 on/off |
| `interface vlan ID` + `ip address A.B.C.D/len` | config-svi | SVI on OVS internal port |
| `ip route PREFIX NEXT_HOP` | config | static route |
| `show running-config` / `show startup-config` | priv | |
| `write` / `write memory` | priv, config | both save running → startup |
| `write erase` | priv | resets startup config (asks confirmation) |
| `show etherchannel summary` | priv | alias of `show port-channel summary` |
| `show terminal monitor` | exec, priv | reports that live log streaming is not implemented |
| `show interfaces description` | exec, priv | port status + description on narrow terminals |
| `show running-config interface NAME` | priv | one stanza, e.g. `sh run int eth1/1` |
| `do EXEC-COMMAND` | config* | e.g. `do show vlan brief` |
| `write memory` / `copy running-config startup-config` | priv | explicit save |
| `show interfaces [status\|counters\|mapping\|NAME]` | priv | |
| `show vlan brief` / `show vlan ID` | priv | |
| `show mac address-table [vlan ID] [interface NAME]` | priv | from OVS FDB |
| `show ip interface brief` / `show ip route` | priv | |
| `show spanning-tree`, `show port-channel summary` | priv | |
| `show lldp neighbors` | priv | errors if `lldpd` missing |
| `show system resources`, `show version` | priv | |
| `show logging`, `show configuration drift` | priv | |
| `rollback [ID]`, `reload` | priv | `reload` asks for confirmation |
| `?` and `TAB` | any | context help / completion |

Abbreviations work when unambiguous (`sh ver`, `conf t`, `int eth 1/1`), and
ambiguous ones are rejected exactly like IOS (`% Ambiguous command: "sh i"`).

### Interface ranges

```
Switch(config)# interface range eth1-4
Switch(config-if-range)# description access ports
Switch(config-if-range)# switchport mode access
Switch(config-if-range)# switchport access vlan 20
Switch(config-if-range)# exit
```

Accepted forms: `eth1-4`, `eth1-eth4`, `eth1,3,5`, `ethernet 1 - 2`, `1-4`.
Commands are applied to every member; a failure on one port is reported with its
name and does not stop the rest.

### Port-channels

A port-channel can be created **before** it has members, exactly like Cisco:

```
Switch(config)# interface port-channel 1
Port-channel 1 created (add members with 'channel-group 1 mode active').
Switch(config-if)# exit
Switch(config)# interface ethernet 1
Switch(config-if)# channel-group 1 mode active
```

The bond appears in Open vSwitch only once the second member joins; with one
member no `add-bond` is attempted (OVS rejects one-interface bonds) and that
port keeps switching on its own. `show port-channel summary` states this
explicitly. `no interface port-channel N` deletes the channel and returns its
members to standalone ports.

## 7a. SSH access: switch CLI on 22, host shell on 8022

After installation the two roles are separated:

| Port | What answers | Accounts |
|------|--------------|----------|
| **22** | MakeMeSwitch CLI (switch) | appliance users (`username … privilege … secret …`), the same as the panel |
| **8022** | the host's own `sshd` | OS users (e.g. `debian`) with the keys/passwords they already had |

```
ssh admin@10.0.0.87          # Switch> ... the switch CLI
ssh debian@10.0.0.87 -p 8022 # normal Linux shell
```

What the switch CLI over SSH **cannot** do, by design: run commands (`exec`),
transfer files (SFTP/SCP), open tunnels (local/remote forwarding), or forward
agents/X11. A logged-in user only ever talks to the CLI, so there is no path
from an appliance account to a root shell.

Before port 22 is touched, the installer moves `sshd` to 8022, proves that
8022 accepts connections, and only then starts the switch SSH service. If any
step fails it restores the original `/etc/ssh/sshd_config` (backed up in
`/etc/makemeswitch/backup/sshd_config.orig`) and leaves host SSH on 22. The
session you are installing from is never disconnected.

Prefer to keep host SSH on 22? Use `--keep-ssh-22`; the switch CLI then lives
only on the local console and the panel terminal.

**Locked out?** From the physical console:

```bash
sudo systemctl stop makemeswitch-ssh
sudo rm -f /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/99-makemeswitch.conf
sudo cp /etc/makemeswitch/backup/sshd_config.orig /etc/ssh/sshd_config
sudo systemctl restart ssh        # host SSH is back on 22
```

Remember to open both ports in any firewall between you and the switch:
`tcp/22` (switch CLI) **and** `tcp/8022` (host admin).

## 7b. Monitoring: SNMP and remote syslog

### SNMP (v2c, read-only)

The agent is installed as `makemeswitch-snmpd.service` and answers on `udp/161`.
Default community: **`public`**, read-only, any source (change it, see below).
It is an honest L2 device, not a Cisco imitation:

| What | Detail |
|------|--------|
| `sysDescr` | `MakeMeSwitch <ver> (Open vSwitch L2 switch on Linux)` |
| `sysObjectID` | `1.3.6.1.4.1.32473.1.1` by default (the IANA "examples" PEN). Set yours with `snmp-server object-id 1.3.6.1.4.1.<your-PEN>.1` |
| `sysServices` | `2` — datalink only, which is exactly what this box does |
| `ifTable` / `ifXTable` | one row per switch port (`Ethernet1…`), 64-bit octet counters, speed, MTU, admin/oper state |
| `BRIDGE-MIB` | `dot1dBaseBridgeAddress`, `dot1dBaseNumPorts`, `dot1dBasePortIfIndex`, and the learned `dot1dTpFdbTable` from the OVS MAC table |

```bash
snmpwalk  -v2c -c public 10.0.0.87 1.3.6.1.2.1.1        # identity
snmpwalk  -v2c -c public 10.0.0.87 1.3.6.1.2.1.2.2.1.2  # port names
snmpbulkwalk -v2c -c public 10.0.0.87 1.3.6.1.2.1.31.1.1.1.6  # 64-bit counters
snmpwalk  -v2c -c public 10.0.0.87 1.3.6.1.2.1.17.4.3.1.2      # MAC table
```

LibreNMS/Infoblox discover it as a normal SNMP device, poll the interfaces and
draw traffic graphs; the bridge tables are what make it behave like a switch
rather than a server. Making it appear as a *specific* vendor model is not
something this project does — there is no Cisco enterprise OID in use anywhere.

Configure:

```
Switch(config)# snmp-server community public ro 10.0.0.0/21
Switch(config)# snmp-server contact noc@example.com
Switch(config)# snmp-server location "rack 3, lab"
Switch(config)# snmp-server object-id 1.3.6.1.4.1.99999.1
Switch(config)# no snmp-server enable
Switch# show snmp
```

An empty source list means "any address". Every list entry is a CIDR; requests
from other addresses are dropped without a response (no information leak).
Only SNMPv2c is implemented — there is no v3/USM, and saying otherwise would be
a lie.

### Remote syslog

Switch events (config commits, logins, link changes, engine errors) are sent to
a collector — Infoblox, LibreNMS, rsyslog, a SIEM:

```
Switch(config)# logging host 10.0.0.10 transport udp port 514 severity info facility local0
Switch(config)# logging host 10.0.0.10 transport tcp port 6514 severity warning
Switch# show logging remote
Switch(config)# no logging host
```

RFC 5424 by default, RFC 3164 optional. When rsyslog is installed the installer
also writes `/etc/rsyslog.d/50-makemeswitch.conf`, so the host's own log lines
are forwarded with the same destination and format. That file is regenerated
whenever the setting changes and removed by `uninstall.sh`.

## 7c. Banners

```
Switch(config)# banner login ^C
  Authorised access only. All activity is logged.
  ^C
Switch(config)# banner motd ^C
  Maintenance window: Sundays 02:00-04:00 UTC
  ^C
Switch# show running-config | section banner
```

The login banner is printed before the prompt, the MOTD after it. Both are
plain text (no markup interpretation) and can be cleared with
`no banner login` / `no banner motd`. The panel shows and edits them under
*Advanced → Monitoring and banner*.

## 7d. User accounts and privileges

The same account database is used by the SSH CLI, the console and the panel.

```
Switch(config)# username admin privilege 15 secret 0 MyNewPassword
Switch(config)# username operator privilege 7 secret 0 AnotherPassword
Switch(config)# username viewer privilege 1 secret 0 ReadOnlyPassword
Switch# show users
Switch(config)# no username viewer
```

| Privilege | Role | Can do |
|-----------|------|--------|
| 15 | admin | everything, including users and credentials |
| 2-14 | operator | configuration changes, no user management |
| 0-1 | readonly | show commands only |

Cisco type 9 hashes can be imported directly, without ever handling plaintext:

```
Switch(config)# username admin privilege 15 secret 9 $9$<salt>$<hash>
```

`$9$…` is a *hash*, not a password. Typing `username admin privilege 15 secret
9 MakeMeSwitch` is rejected with a message explaining that a type 9 hash was
expected and that plaintext needs `secret 0 <text>` — the value is never treated
as a password and never echoed back. `show running-config` prints
`secret <redacted>`; hashes, plaintext and secrets are also stripped from the
CLI history file. The last admin account cannot be deleted or demoted, and you
cannot delete the account you are logged in with.

## 8. Configuration lifecycle

- `running-config` lives in SQLite and is what `show running-config` renders.
- `startup-config` is written only by `write memory` / `copy run start`.
- On service start (`Engine.boot`) the **startup config wins**: unsaved running
  changes are discarded and logged as an event. That is what makes `write
  memory` meaningful — the behaviour is enforced, not just documented.
- Every apply is validated first, diffed, then reconciled. On backend failure
  OVS is restored from a snapshot and the running config is unchanged.
- `show configuration drift` compares the desired config with live OVS and
  reports ports that were changed behind MakeMeSwitch's back.

## 9. Web panel

Pages: Dashboard (hostname, versions, uptime, CPU/RAM, OVS status, port counts,
live traffic chart, recent events), **Ports** (clickable front-panel view with
per-port editor), Interfaces, VLANs (+ membership matrix), MAC table,
Topology (LLDP or explicit "neighbour unknown"), Monitoring (RX/TX, packets,
errors, ranges), Configuration (run/start diff, save, export/import, validate,
rollback), Terminal (real CLI over a PTY), Advanced (bonding, STP, LLDP, SPAN,
L3), Logs, Users.

- Dark theme by default, light theme toggle.
- Real-time port state and counters via `/api/v1/ws/stats`.
- All mutations go through the same `Engine` as the CLI, so a CLI change shows
  up in the panel and vice versa.
- The terminal is the actual `makemeswitch` CLI in a PTY with the logged-in
  user's role — never a raw root shell.

### REST API

Binds `0.0.0.0:8000` by default so it is reachable from the LAN like any switch
management UI. Authenticate with `POST /api/v1/auth/login` (cookie + CSRF token).
For anything beyond a trusted management network, enable HTTPS and restrict the
port with a firewall. `GET /docs` serves the OpenAPI UI.

```
GET  /api/v1/system            GET  /api/v1/interfaces      GET  /api/v1/vlans
GET  /api/v1/mac-table         GET  /api/v1/config          GET  /api/v1/stats
POST /api/v1/vlans             PATCH/DELETE /api/v1/vlans/{id}
PATCH /api/v1/interfaces/{name} (name URL-encoded, e.g. Ethernet1%2F1)
POST /api/v1/interfaces/{name}/attach|detach
POST /api/v1/config/validate   POST /api/v1/config/apply    POST /api/v1/config/save
GET  /api/v1/config/drift      POST /api/v1/config/preview  POST /api/v1/config/rollback
POST /api/v1/auth/login        POST /api/v1/auth/logout      GET /api/v1/auth/session
WS   /api/v1/ws/stats
```

## 10. Limitations

- **RSTP convergence.** Ports created while RSTP is already running can stay
  blocked for the 15 s forwarding delay. MakeMeSwitch works around this by
  creating ports with RSTP paused and enabling RSTP at the end of the apply;
  if you change OVS by hand you may still see the delay.
- **Host firewall.** Inter-VLAN routing traverses the kernel `FORWARD` chain.
  Docker sets its policy to `DROP`, so MakeMeSwitch installs a small
  `OSW-FORWARD` chain (jump rule at the top of `FORWARD`) that accepts only
  traffic to/from its own SVI interfaces. Other firewall rules are untouched.
- **USB Ethernet.** These adapters often re-enumerate on replug and some report
  bogus speed/duplex. The stable USB path mapping mitigates renames, but a
  different adapter in the same port is reported as a mismatch, never silently
  remapped.
- **OVS counters** refresh every few seconds, so bandwidth graphs use a 4 s
  window and can lag slightly.
- **MAC aging** is OVS's default (300 s); it is not configurable from the CLI
  yet.
- **No ACL/QoS/storm-control.** Not exposed anywhere, because OVS needs
  OpenFlow rules for those and we do not ship half-working buttons.
- **L3 is IPv4-only** and static-only; FRRouting integration is a stub.
- **SNMPv2c only.** No v3/USM, no SNMP sets (read-only agent), no traps. LLDP
  information is available through `lldpcli`, not through LLDP-MIB over SNMP.
- **SNMP counters come from the kernel** (`/sys/class/net/<if>/statistics`), so
  they are per-physical-port and 32-bit values wrap in `ifTable`; use
  `ifXTable`/`ifHCInOctets` for graphs.
- **The switch SSH service speaks the CLI only.** No `scp`/`sftp`/`rsync` onto
  the appliance: use the host account on port 8022 for file transfer.
- **The panel and the CLI assume you own the box.** They run as root (they must,
  to program OVS); the mitigation is that no shell reaches users through them.
- Not tested with bonded *management* links or with NetworkManager actively
  managing candidate ports — detach the NIC from the host manager first.

## 11. Testing

```bash
# unit tests (no root, fake OVS backend)
.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/unit -q

# integration tests (root + OVS + network namespaces)
sudo .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/integration -q
```

Integration tests create disposable `veth` pairs inside network namespaces,
attach them to a dedicated bridge (`oswtest`) and verify **real forwarding**:

| Test | Verifies |
|---|---|
| `test_same_vlan_forwards_and_isolates` | same-VLAN ping works, cross-VLAN ping fails, MAC table populated |
| `test_administrative_shutdown_blocks_forwarding` | `shutdown` stops forwarding, `no shutdown` restores it |
| `test_trunk_preserves_vlan_tags` | tagged VLAN 10 crosses a trunk to a `vlan` sub-interface |
| `test_configuration_survives_restart` | startup config is re-applied after OVS state is wiped |
| `test_running_config_drift_detected` | manual OVS change shows up as drift |
| `test_layer3_svi_routes_between_vlans` | SVIs route between VLAN 10 and VLAN 20 in both directions |

Last run on this host (Debian 12, OVS 3.1.0):

```
tests/unit         48 passed
tests/integration   6 passed
```

Unit coverage includes parser modes/abbreviations, model validation, engine
idempotency/confirmation/rollback, SQLite persistence, CLI↔store sync and the
REST API (auth, CSRF, RBAC, VLAN/interface operations).

## 12. Troubleshooting

**The port table shows `ovs-system` / `mms0` / `Ethernet4096`.** Those were
registered as ports by builds before 0.1.3 (the OVS bridge and its internal
interface both carry a MAC, so they looked like NICs). They are pruned
automatically the first time the engine syncs after the upgrade — restart the
service (`sudo systemctl restart makemeswitch`) and reload the panel.

**A new NIC is not listed.** Boot discovery rescans hardware independently of
old mappings. Reboot after adding NICs, then check
`sudo systemctl status makemeswitch-nicnames` and `ip -br link`.
If 0.1.4 collapsed multiple NICs onto one database identity, 0.1.5 recovers each
physical NIC, but configuration already overwritten by the old collision
cannot be reconstructed; verify per-port VLANs against your backup.


| Symptom | Check |
|---|---|
| `% OVS is not available` | `systemctl status openvswitch-switch`, `ovs-vsctl show` |
| Port stays down | `ip link show <nic>`; USB NIC unplugged? `show interfaces mapping` |
| No traffic between two access ports | `show vlan brief`, `show mac address-table`, `show configuration drift` |
| Trunk carries nothing | allowed VLAN list must include the tag; native VLAN must exist |
| Panel shows `Web panel is not built` | assets missing for the running unit: check `ls /opt/makemeswitch/app/makemeswitch/web/dist` and `systemctl show makemeswitch -p Environment`; re-run `sudo ./install.sh` (it rebuilds and verifies) |
| `makemeswitch: command not found` | `sudo ln -sf /opt/makemeswitch/venv/bin/makemeswitch /usr/local/bin/makemeswitch` |
| Panel unreachable remotely | check `ss -ltnp \| grep 8000` — bound to `127.0.0.1`? set `api.bind: 0.0.0.0` in `/etc/makemeswitch/makemeswitch.yaml` and restart; also check the host firewall |
| Inter-VLAN routing blocked | `iptables -S FORWARD` — look for the `OSW-FORWARD` jump |
| Login loop | cookies blocked, or service restarted (sessions are DB-backed but expire) |
| After OVS upgrade | `systemctl restart makemeswitch`, then `show configuration drift` |

Logs: `journalctl -u makemeswitch -f` and `/var/log/makemeswitch/*.log` (JSON lines).
Audit trail of every change: `show logging` or the Logs page.

## 13. Uninstall

```bash
sudo ./uninstall.sh            # stop service, keep config and database
sudo ./uninstall.sh --purge    # also remove /opt/makemeswitch and /etc/makemeswitch
sudo ./uninstall.sh --purge --purge-data   # also remove /var/lib/makemeswitch
```

The bridge is only deleted when it has no ports, unless `--force` is given —
removing a bridge with live ports would disconnect the host. Host networking
configuration is never modified.

## 14. Roadmap

1. **Now (done):** L2 MVP, CLI, persistence, web panel, static L3, tests.
2. **Next:** OpenFlow ACLs, QoS/queues, storm control, MAC aging config,
   per-port `show interfaces counters errors`.
3. **Then:** FRRouting integration (OSPF/BGP) with real `vtysh` config
   generation, IPv6 SVIs, VRRP.
4. **Later:** Ansible collection, Prometheus exporter, config templates,
   multi-switch stacking over LLDP.

